      "path": "app/scanner/metadata.py",
      "content": "Unsupported storage never receives `UNSUPPORTED_STORAGE`: `parse_metadata()` raises `ValueError` for every non-`sqlite3` identifier, and this handler immediately converts it to `BAD_METADATA`. For example, otherwise valid metadata with `storage_identifier: mcap` is persisted under the wrong reason code; the later storage check cannot be reached for that input. Distinguish this parse failure explicitly (e.g. with a dedicated exception) while preserving `validate_bag_input()`'s raising behavior.",
      "existing_code": "    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001\n        return None, \"BAD_METADATA\", str(exc)[:512]",
      "start_line": 72,
      "end_line": 73,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/scanner/metadata.py",
      "content": "Malformed DB3 path element types escape classification. Metadata with `storage_identifier: sqlite3`, `message_count: 1`, and `relative_file_paths: [null]` parses successfully, but `_check_parsed()` calls `Path(None)`, which raises `TypeError`, not `ValueError`. The runner consequently counts the bag as failed on every scan instead of reporting and persisting `BAD_METADATA`. Validate that path elements are strings before checking them, or handle the input-type failure here without changing the legacy validator's contract.",
      "existing_code": "    try:\n        _check_parsed(parsed, entries)\n    except ValueError as exc:",
      "start_line": 84,
      "end_line": 86,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/scanner/metadata.py",
      "content": "Do not infer the failure category from a substring of the full error message: it includes metadata-controlled filenames. For example, `relative_file_paths: ['../missing files.db3']` causes `_check_parsed()` to raise `metadata contains invalid DB3 paths: ../missing files.db3`, but this branch reports `MISSING_DB3` instead of `BAD_METADATA`. That incorrect reason is then persisted in the rejection evidence. Use distinct exception types/reason codes, or at least match the fixed error prefix rather than arbitrary message content.",
      "suggestion_code": "        if str(exc).startswith(\"metadata references missing files: \"):\n            return None, \"MISSING_DB3\", str(exc)[:512]",
      "existing_code": "        if \"missing files\" in str(exc):\n            return None, \"MISSING_DB3\", str(exc)[:512]",
      "start_line": 87,
      "end_line": 88,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/scanner/metadata.py",
      "content": "Do not persist the raw YAML exception text as rejection detail. PyYAML syntax errors include a source-line excerpt; for example, malformed input `password: supersecret: value` places the password in `str(exc)`. The runner sends this detail to the backend, which stores it in rejection evidence and returns it in submission responses. Truncation to 512 characters does not redact that content. Use a sanitized error category and line/column coordinates without source excerpts (and avoid echoing arbitrary scalar values from conversion errors).",
